course: world
lesson: 18
title: Materials and their properties
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every material has properties: hard or soft, stiff or bendy, see-through or not, waterproof or absorbent. We choose a material for a job because of its properties.
explain: Heat moves easily through some materials, called conductors of heat, such as metals; it moves slowly through insulators, such as wood, plastic, wool, cork and air trapped in tiny pockets.
explain: An object floats if it is less dense than the water: it has less mass than the same amount of water would have. A steel ship floats because its hollow shape is full of air and pushes aside a great deal of water.
explain: Some solids dissolve in water: sugar and salt break up into particles too small to see and spread through the water, making a solution. Sand and pepper do not dissolve.
explain: For example, a metal spoon left in hot tea soon has a hot handle, but a wooden spoon does not, because metal conducts heat and wood insulates.
fact: Metals are good conductors of heat and of electricity.
fact: Wood, plastic, wool, cork and still air are poor conductors of heat, called insulators.
fact: A warm coat does not make heat; it slows down the loss of your own body heat.
fact: Insulators also keep cold things cold, like food in a cool box.
fact: Double-glazed windows have two sheets, or panes, of glass with a layer of trapped air between them.
fact: An object floats if it is less dense than water, and sinks if it is denser.
fact: Whether an object floats depends on its material and its shape, not on its weight alone; things with air inside them, like an apple, are less dense and float more easily.
fact: A dissolved solid is still there: a solution weighs as much as the water and the solid together.
fact: Sugar dissolves faster in warm water and when it is stirred, and more of it can dissolve in hot water than in cold.
fact: Sand, pepper and cooking oil do not dissolve in water.
fact: Dissolving can be reversed: you can get salt back by letting the water evaporate.
exercise: Why are saucepans made of metal but given plastic or wooden handles? | The metal passes heat to the food and the handle insulates your hand | Metal is lighter than plastic or wood | Plastic handles make the food cook faster | Wooden handles carry the heat out of the pan and away into the kitchen air
exercise: Why does a woolly hat keep your head warm in winter? | It traps air and slows the loss of your body heat | Wool makes heat of its own | Wool lets the cold air through quickly | Wool soaks up warmth from the Sun and gives it back later
exercise: A big steel ship floats, but a small steel nail sinks. Why? | The ship's hollow shape holds air and pushes aside lots of water | Ships are made of a special light steel | Ships float only because they keep moving forward through the water | The sea is always deeper under a ship
exercise: Which spoon should you use to stir hot soup so your fingers stay cool? | A wooden spoon | A steel spoon | A silver spoon | An aluminium spoon
exercise: Sugar is stirred into tea and can no longer be seen. Where is it? | Spread through the tea as tiny particles | It has disappeared completely | It sank and hides under the cup | It has turned into water
exercise: How could you make sugar dissolve faster? | Use warm water and stir it | Use cold water and keep it still | Use big lumps instead of grains | Put the cup into the freezer
exercise: What is the best thing to carry ice cream home in on a hot day? | A thick insulated cool bag | A thin metal tin | Nothing, so the breeze can cool it | A thin paper bag with holes
exercise: A child wraps a snowman in a warm coat to keep it from feeling cold. What happens? | It melts more slowly, because the coat keeps the warm air out | It melts faster, because the coat makes heat | It melts at once in the warm coat | Nothing, because coats only work on living things that make heat
exercise: Which of these floats in water? | A piece of cork | A metal door key | A small glass marble | A small, smooth stone
exercise: A heavy log floats but a light pin sinks. What does this show? | Floating depends on density, not on weight alone | Heavy things always float | Light things always sink because they are small | Light things always sink because they are so small and thin
exercise: You stir sand into a glass of water. What happens? | The sand does not dissolve and settles at the bottom | The sand dissolves just like sugar | The sand turns into mud for ever | The sand turns the water into a solid block after a while
exercise: 50 g of salt is dissolved in 500 g of water. How much does the salty water weigh? | 550 g | 500 g | 450 g | 50 g
exercise: Why do double-glazed windows help keep a house warm? | The air trapped between the panes is an insulator | The two sheets of glass make heat between them | Glass is a very good conductor of heat | They let more sunlight in at night
exercise: On a cold morning the metal bars of a climbing frame feel colder than a wooden bench, though both are at the same temperature. Why? | Metal carries heat away from your hand faster | The metal is really much colder than the wood | Wood makes a little warmth of its own | Metal soaks up the cold from the air
test: A cook leaves a metal spoon and a plastic spoon in a pot of hot stew. Which handle gets hot? | The metal one | The plastic one | Both get equally hot | Neither of them
test: Why do birds fluff out their feathers on a frosty day? | To trap more air, which insulates them | To catch the warm wind | To let the cold air reach right down to their skin | To make their feathers dry faster
test: A ball of plasticine sinks, but the same plasticine pressed into a boat shape floats. Why? | The boat shape pushes aside enough water to hold it up | The plasticine became lighter | Boats can only float on salty water | The water under the boat is warmer
test: Which is true about salt dissolved in water? | The salt is still in the water | The salt has been destroyed | The salt has turned into water | The salt has floated away as a gas
test: Why are picnic cool boxes made with a thick layer of foam? | Foam is full of trapped air and insulates | Foam makes cold of its own | Foam lets heat through quickly | Foam is heavy and holds the lid down
test: How can you get salt back from salty water? | Let the water evaporate | Pour it through filter paper | Stir it very fast | Pour it through a sieve
test: A builder puts a thick fluffy layer of insulation in a roof. What is it for? | To stop heat escaping from the house in winter | To make the roof waterproof | To make the rooms brighter | To make the roof heavier in the wind
test: An apple floats but a raw potato of the same size usually sinks. Why? | The apple is less dense, partly because of the air inside it | The apple is lighter in colour | The potato is rounder in shape | The apple has a stalk at the top that holds it up in the water
test: Which will dissolve in a glass of warm water? | Salt | Sand | Cooking oil | Small pebbles
test: why's the metal spoon in my tea so hot but not the wooden one | Metal is a good conductor of heat, wood is not | Wood is a good conductor of heat | The metal spoon makes its own heat | Tea only heats up shiny things
test: will sugar dissolve quicker in hot tea or cold | In hot tea | In cold tea | Just the same in both | It won't dissolve in either
test: Leo says a coat keeps u warm cos it makes heat. true? | No, it slows the loss of your own body heat | Yes, wool makes heat when you wear it | Yes, coats warm up in the sunshine and keep it | No, it keeps you warm by keeping out the light
test: Tom reckons heavy things always sink. right? | No, it depends on material and shape, not weight alone | Yes, anything heavy goes to the bottom | Yes, ships float only because they are light | No, heavy things always float
exam: Identify, from among the following, the material that conducts heat most effectively. | Metal | Dry wood | Woollen cloth | Natural cork
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of a warm coat? | It retards the loss of the wearer's own body heat | It generates heat of its own | It draws warmth from the surrounding air | It admits the heat of the Sun
exam: Identify the determining condition by which an object floats upon water. | Its being less dense than water | Its being light in weight alone | Its being composed of wood alone | Its being of small dimensions
exam: It is given that 10 grams of sugar is dissolved in 100 grams of water. Ascertain the mass of the resulting solution. | One hundred and ten grams | Precisely one hundred grams | Ninety grams, the sugar having vanished | Ten grams, being the sugar alone
